<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <!--
    Explicit manifest-level DPI awareness for this WPF app.

    ApplicationHighDpiMode in the .csproj turned out to be a no-op here: that MSBuild property
    only takes effect by injecting a Application.SetHighDpiMode(...) call into a WinForms-
    generated Main() - but this project's actual entry point is WPF-generated (App.Main(), per
    the App.xaml.cs partial class), since UseWPF wins entry-point generation over
    UseWindowsForms even with both set true (UseWindowsForms here is only for the tray icon's
    NotifyIcon). No manifest or bootstrap code was ever produced by that property (confirmed:
    no .manifest file appears anywhere under obj/ after a build), so the DPI-virtualized-
    resolution bug (VirtualDisplayManager.FindVirtualOutput reading the virtual display's true
    2560x1600 as 2048x1280 via un-aware GDI calls) kept recurring inconsistently regardless of
    that setting.

    This is the actual, WPF-correct mechanism: Windows reads and applies dpiAwareness from the
    embedded manifest at process creation, before any managed code - including the App
    class's own static initializers - ever runs. Referenced via <ApplicationManifest> in the
    .csproj.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
